WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.159 --> 00:00:04.159
[Subtitles by Camilla Kristensen]
Moderator (M): "Andy, we're gonna start with

00:00:04.159 --> 00:00:05.159
you"
Andy Serkis (AS): "Oh my god"

00:00:05.159 --> 00:00:06.159
M: "If that's alright?

00:00:06.159 --> 00:00:11.040
You've been involved in both sides of this,
of course this whole journey of being behind

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:12.269
the camera and in front of it.

00:00:12.269 --> 00:00:17.270
What are you gonna take away from this experience
and from being able to do both sides of those

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:18.270
things?"

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:19.270
AS: "I'm...

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:20.630
It really is... it's very much...

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:25.500
this whole journey for me it's... extraord...
been extraordinary... both as a... working

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:26.550
on a new technology.

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:29.599
Again, this kind of goes back to where Pete
comes from, as a film maker, and his desire

00:00:29.599 --> 00:00:36.790
to push the envelope, and to turn, you know,
reach a point in filmmaking where visual effects

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:41.070
suddenly becomes character, so the whole performance
capture journey's been extraordinary from

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:45.050
that point of view, but then also to be asked
to direct the second unit was an enormous

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:52.200
shock but... it really speaks to Pete's understanding
of... and the way that he collaborates with

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:56.079
all of the cast members and all of the crew,
and as a natural leader and as a brilliant

00:00:56.079 --> 00:01:03.040
film maker he's, in terms of being, you know,
without overselling him, 'cause he doesn't

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:09.910
need selling really, but he is... his virtues
are that he is the most incredibly generous

00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:14.020
human being, and I think to learn from a director,
who is an incredibly generous human being,

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:16.580
you couldn't ever want a better mentor/teacher"
Peter Jackson (PJ): "I think I'm gonna have

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:22.650
to hire you again then"
AS: "It's a deal"

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:24.620
M: "Lee.

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:32.010
You kind of steal the show with your horseback
entry and this sort of thing..."

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:35.500
Evangeline Lilly (EL): "Elkback"
M: "Sorry elkback, yeah.

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:38.050
It kind of adds to his whole kind of presence
and grandeur doesn't it?

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:39.770
Is it fun to kind of play with?

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:42.490
To kind of even... make him even more kind
of powerful and grandiose?

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:47.340
Lee Pace (LP): "Oh I mean on set that is basically:
"okay, put on this blond wig, put on this

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:51.410
silver armor, and you're gonna sit here",
and I'm like: "what's next" and he goes, "you're

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:55.840
gonna ride an elk into a battle of five armies;
there'll be orks here and...", and you kind

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:01.470
of just go for it, swing your sword around.

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:08.090
And then... and then... and then show up last
night and watch the movie back, and it's just

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:09.090
extraordinary.

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:12.920
This battle is just massive and somehow I
find myself in it"

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:14.710
M: "Orlando"
Orlando Bloom (OB): "Yeah"

00:02:14.710 --> 00:02:16.210
M: "How are you sir?"

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:18.000
OB: "Hello love, how are you?"

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.070
M: "This must have been an emotional journey
for you.

00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:25.500
From being, you know from being there across
the kind of two epics"

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:27.730
OB: "Yeah, yeah it is.

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:33.350
It's been a crazy... crazy to think that I
was 21 when I got casted and moved out to

00:02:33.350 --> 00:02:38.520
New Zealand, and I'm 37 now, so puts it slightly
in perspective, but yeah...

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:39.800
It is, it's bitter sweet, it's... there's...

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:44.310
I hope Pete feels a huge sense of achievement
at this point, 'cause I'm feeling a little

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:49.040
bit of that and very grateful to just have
been on the ride really"

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:50.360
M: "Evangeline.

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:51.360
What a character.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:52.780
She has no fear; she fears nothing.

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:53.780
Would you agree?"

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:55.350
EL: "I totally disagree.

00:02:55.350 --> 00:03:00.490
I think that she's terrified of everything
and that she overcomes those fears, and does

00:03:00.490 --> 00:03:02.210
it anyway"
M: "She hides it brilliantly"

00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:03.940
EL: "Oh great"
M: "You hide it brilliantly"

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:05.790
EL: "That's what elves are supposed to do.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:09.190
You don't show anything on the outside, it's
all inside"

00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:11.240
M: "Richard.

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:13.220
What a journey he goes on in this film.

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:16.550
Was that great to be able to take and to kind
of those depths"

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:19.319
Richard Armitage (RA): "Yeah, I'm trying to
go through this press tour without repeating

00:03:19.319 --> 00:03:25.360
the word 'journey' or 'story arc', but really,
Thorin has such an interesting story arc to

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:29.280
play over three movies, and when does one
ever get the opportunity to play a single

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:34.620
story through three pieces, and you know the
material is incredible, Tolkein is incredible,

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:36.770
and the screenplay is absolutely fantastic.

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:41.500
I was given some amazing dialog to say, which
I'm very very grateful for"

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:46.950
Philippa Boyens (PB): "Good answer Richard"
M: "Peter.

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:52.430
Sir, you said that you have a responsibility
with these films, and these characters I guess

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:53.430
as well.

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:56.780
How do you look at that responsibility now
that it's kind of finished and you've delivered

00:03:56.780 --> 00:03:57.780
these stories?"

00:03:57.780 --> 00:03:59.849
PJ: "I don't have the responsibility any more.

00:03:59.849 --> 00:04:06.800
I can go to the beach"
EL: "As you can tell, he does that a lot"

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:11.481
PJ: "But I mean, any filmmmaker's responsibility
because you are spending, you know, you're

00:04:11.481 --> 00:04:15.090
spending money that is not yours, and you
know, so you've got to be responsible for

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:16.090
that.

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:17.090
You've...

00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:20.880
I mean, I also feel very responsible for the
fact that you're trying to entertain people,

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:26.520
and for me utter failure, is to make a film
that people go to, and they pay the money

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:30.570
to go see it, and they don't like it, and
so, you know, from that point of view, I'm

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:34.880
sure there are people that have seen these
films and they don't like them, but the majority

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:40.070
of people, certainly from what we can see,
the majority of people have gone and enjoyed

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:46.870
them which is, for me, that's why we do what
we do, isn't it?

00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:49.170
To give people a good time at the movies.

00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:50.170
So yeah..."

00:04:50.170 --> 00:04:51.170
M: "Martin.

00:04:51.170 --> 00:04:53.420
Hello"
Martin Freeman (MF): "What?!

00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:54.420
Christ!"

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:57.260
M: "So rude"
MF: "On and on and on"

00:04:57.260 --> 00:05:01.370
M: "I've gotta get down the line and speed
it up a bit.

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:06.870
Not many times an actor gets the chance to
revisit a character on more than one occasion..."

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:08.945
MF: "Yes"
M: "What does it allow you to do then as an

00:05:08.945 --> 00:05:09.945
actor?

00:05:09.945 --> 00:05:12.380
Does it kind of allow you more freedom or
is it an easier or harder opportunity as an

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:13.380
actor?

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:17.840
MF: "Well ehm, it gives you to opportunity
to actually grow and expand inside what you

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:23.000
are doing, so I love to expand inside of Bilbo
over a course of three years...

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:24.000
What?!...

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:25.000
And ehm...

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:29.300
That's the truth, and I'm only telling the
truth and now I'm being persecuted for it,

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:33.210
and I think we know another man that happened
to, don't we?

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:35.100
No but we...

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:38.870
we"
Ian McKellen (IM): "Jesus Christ!"

00:05:38.870 --> 00:05:43.670
M: "This is not the time or the place"
MF: "No I'm just saying, but within that...

00:05:43.670 --> 00:05:44.670
yeah we all have...

00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:45.670
A long time...

00:05:45.670 --> 00:05:46.720
I was gonna be so good on this.

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:51.180
Yeah we have a long time to play and discover
things our characters do, we don't just have

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:55.699
five minutes, you have a long time to sort
of play this, and if it's not your time to...

00:05:55.699 --> 00:05:59.380
'cause I was always having the conversation
with Peter, when do I get to be angry, you

00:05:59.380 --> 00:06:02.310
know, when do I get to sort of show that different
side, and he would always be saying: "it's

00:06:02.310 --> 00:06:05.040
coming, it's coming, don't worry, pace yourself"
you know"

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:06.699
MF: "Exactly, you have to shape it out.

00:06:06.699 --> 00:06:11.460
What you learn is to pace yourself, and you
learn to... yeah you learn patience basically,

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:14.990
so that time we want to play that and you
want to show that about that character, it

00:06:14.990 --> 00:06:18.770
will come, but you just have to wait, and
it's yeah... it's... yes"

00:06:18.770 --> 00:06:20.090
M: "Thank you.

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:24.060
Sir Ian McKellen"
OJ: "Very good... very good answer"

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:27.120
MF: "Thanks very much"
PJ: "No I thought...

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:28.120
I quite enjoyed it"
MF: "Thank you"

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:29.120
M: "Are you finished Martin?"

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:30.630
MF: "Yep"
M: "Sir Ian McKellen.

00:06:30.630 --> 00:06:34.100
Do you think you'll ever say goodbye, farewell
to Gandalf?"

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:38.979
PB: "Do you think you'll ever say goodbye
to Gandalf?"

00:06:38.979 --> 00:06:40.340
M: "Do you want me to...

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:41.610
I'll shout"
IM: "Oh you see, the thing is, no....

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:45.770
I said goodbye to Gandalf in 2000 when I left
New Zealand.

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:53.690
Well here we are, 2014, it goes on and on
and on, but... and now... and has been the

00:06:53.690 --> 00:06:55.310
blessing of my professional life.

00:06:55.310 --> 00:06:56.310
I'm...

00:06:56.310 --> 00:06:57.310
I'm...

00:06:57.310 --> 00:06:59.600
Like all the other actors we think we are
so bloody lucky.

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:06.090
There's nothing about luck in the making of
the films, of cause that's the planning.

00:07:06.090 --> 00:07:09.170
The chance that we were selected...

00:07:09.170 --> 00:07:10.870
well... wonderfully...

00:07:10.870 --> 00:07:11.870
wonderful.

00:07:11.870 --> 00:07:18.210
But I was just impressed last night by the
age of the kids who'd slept out all night

00:07:18.210 --> 00:07:19.210
to come and wish us well.

00:07:19.210 --> 00:07:24.920
And some of them were born in the last century
when we started...

00:07:24.920 --> 00:07:32.790
not born, so our work is part of their lives,
and you know, what are we doing it for other

00:07:32.790 --> 00:07:34.479
than to have an effect.

00:07:34.479 --> 00:07:40.590
And the effect could be that crucial to them,
and they're now gonna show the films to their

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:43.290
own kids.

00:07:43.290 --> 00:07:51.060
To have been involved in films that are now
classics is overwhelming, you can't take any

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:53.540
credit for it, it just happened.

00:07:53.540 --> 00:07:58.320
So in the end...

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:03.140
it's not the end, it's the beginning now,
of people seeing the film, and then we'd want

00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:09.350
to see the six of them and that would be a
whole other new experience, none of us has

00:08:09.350 --> 00:08:11.100
been able to have, until this last Hobbit
was made"

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:16.820
M: "Thank you"
PJ: "24 hours of joy.

00:08:16.820 --> 00:08:19.970
We are... just to expand slightly on something
Ian was saying.

00:08:19.970 --> 00:08:26.130
I was just thinking last night that we're
about 3 or 4 years away from the generation

00:08:26.130 --> 00:08:29.820
that will see these films, the six movies,
in the order that they should see them in;

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:32.529
the story order rather than the order they
were shot.

00:08:32.529 --> 00:08:36.729
'Cause you know children that are too young
now, like 3 years old, 4 years old, 5 years

00:08:36.729 --> 00:08:40.559
old, are too young to see these films, in
a few years, a very short time, they will

00:08:40.559 --> 00:08:46.740
be able to see them, and hopefully they will
see them from the Hobbit 1 through to the

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:51.490
Return of the King, so we're very soon, we're
not gonna have this sort of back-to-front

00:08:51.490 --> 00:08:58.149
thing, it'll be like... it will exist as a...
as the six film, you know, story that it should

00:08:58.149 --> 00:08:59.149
be so... yeah..."

00:08:59.149 --> 00:09:00.149
M: "Was that what you planned from the start?"

00:09:00.149 --> 00:09:03.779
PJ: "Well not in The Lord of the Rings, I
didn't know I was gonna do The Hobbit, but

00:09:03.779 --> 00:09:08.429
when we knew we were gonna do The Hobbit,
we were always thinking of the full arc of

00:09:08.429 --> 00:09:15.480
the story, 'cause there's a lot of stuff in
The Hobbit that we did... that we planted

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:19.641
which is designed to, you know, for people
that do finally see it in the correct order,

00:09:19.641 --> 00:09:20.641
it'll make sense"
M: "Can I bring in Philippa, on the other

00:09:20.641 --> 00:09:21.850
side of Sir Ian.

00:09:21.850 --> 00:09:23.410
Hi Philippa"
PB: "Hi"

00:09:23.410 --> 00:09:30.819
M: "I mean, all the foundations we hear in
terms of, you know, you kind of with the films

00:09:30.819 --> 00:09:35.540
that has gone before this, but does that make
it easier in terms of kind of knowing where

00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:39.080
the end point is, for you in terms of this
final film?"

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:41.579
PB: "Having done The Lord of the Rings first
you mean?"

00:09:41.579 --> 00:09:43.139
M: "Yeah"
PB: "Definitely.

00:09:43.139 --> 00:09:49.111
I think it was luck that we actually did,
I think, those films first because I think

00:09:49.111 --> 00:09:52.970
we would have made a very different Hobbit
if we'd started with The Hobbit, probably

00:09:52.970 --> 00:09:59.389
more a children's tale, uhm, and uhm, and
you know, not so say that that's would have

00:09:59.389 --> 00:10:03.300
been necessarily a bad thing, but we always
knew, as Peter said, that these was gonna

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:07.839
set against a backdrop of films that were
already in existence, and what I do love now

00:10:07.839 --> 00:10:15.699
is that we've seen, for example, the relationship
of Gandalf and Galadriel and that she comes

00:10:15.699 --> 00:10:21.780
to save him and... so when she is told in
Fellowship of the Ring that he has died, it's

00:10:21.780 --> 00:10:25.129
gonna play in a completely different way.

00:10:25.129 --> 00:10:29.199
So that's the experience that I think is gonna
be special for those people who couldn't see

00:10:29.199 --> 00:10:32.249
it in that order"
M: "Thank you.

00:10:32.249 --> 00:10:35.249
Luke"
Luke Evans (LE): "Hey ya"

00:10:35.249 --> 00:10:36.320
M: "Bard the bowman..."

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:38.480
LE: "Bard the lonely bowman"
M: "Everyone needs a bowman to save them.

00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:40.170
Was it fun playing the hero?"

00:10:40.170 --> 00:10:42.170
LE: "Haha, eh yeah.

00:10:42.170 --> 00:10:49.249
He didn't.... he wasn't a hero when I first
read him on the page, he was just the lonely

00:10:49.249 --> 00:10:50.269
bargeman.

00:10:50.269 --> 00:10:58.230
I know what he was capable of and what he
ends up doing in the third movie, but when

00:10:58.230 --> 00:11:07.779
I started playing him, all those years ago,
he was a father of three without a wife and,

00:11:07.779 --> 00:11:13.499
you know, he had a long, long journey ahead
of him, but it was very satisfying last night

00:11:13.499 --> 00:11:20.959
to actually see the final... the look of that
rooftop sequence which was the first thing

00:11:20.959 --> 00:11:22.540
I ever shot, wasn't it Pete?"

00:11:22.540 --> 00:11:24.100
PJ: "Yes it was"
LE: "Yeah.

00:11:24.100 --> 00:11:28.300
He put me of wires and sent me up a rooftop
for four days"

00:11:28.300 --> 00:11:33.209
PJ: "There's a lot of scenes in this movie,
this Battle of the Five armies, that was shot

00:11:33.209 --> 00:11:37.829
four years ago, which is a very strange performance"
LE: "That was my... that was four years ago,

00:11:37.829 --> 00:11:40.699
I must have shot that, yeah, so I waited a
long time to see that scene"

00:11:40.699 --> 00:11:42.879
PJ: "That was something of a bell wasn't it?"

00:11:42.879 --> 00:11:47.899
LE: "It was a very big bell yeah"
M: "Ryan, what a great character you have

00:11:47.899 --> 00:11:48.899
in Alfrid"
Ryan: "Thank you"

00:11:48.899 --> 00:11:52.829
M: "He really helps to set the tone, I think
as well throughout the films, in terms of..."

00:11:52.829 --> 00:11:54.019
Someone: "lowers"
Ryan Gage (RG): "He lowers the tone"

00:11:54.019 --> 00:12:00.889
M: "He does yeah, I wasn't gonna say that
but ehm... but in terms of... did you really

00:12:00.889 --> 00:12:02.220
enjoy kind of...

00:12:02.220 --> 00:12:03.569
I guess kind of bringing him into life.

00:12:03.569 --> 00:12:05.809
Did you get to play with kind of how...

00:12:05.809 --> 00:12:08.920
how... where you took him I guess, in terms
of how that character..."

00:12:08.920 --> 00:12:13.110
RG: "Yeah, I mean it was very clear on the
page that he sort of jumps off the page to

00:12:13.110 --> 00:12:17.220
me, from the first reading of it but ehm...
yeah we got to play around...

00:12:17.220 --> 00:12:22.040
I mean it was a good opportunity for Pete
to be... to play with me and bully me a bit

00:12:22.040 --> 00:12:24.000
and throw eggs at me.

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:29.389
So there was great fun to be had, with him
because he has a sort of foolish nature, he's

00:12:29.389 --> 00:12:35.869
a sort of dark clownish character, so I enjoyed
being able to find a little bit of me that's

00:12:35.869 --> 00:12:41.430
a fool and a bit of me that is vain"
PJ: "There's quite a lot in there actually"

00:12:41.430 --> 00:12:42.630
RG: "Thank you.

00:12:42.630 --> 00:12:47.899
And a bit of me that is sort of bitter and
greedy and all those things and exaggerate

00:12:47.899 --> 00:12:51.949
it, and have fun being that person for a while,
so it was a great treat to be him"

00:12:51.949 --> 00:12:53.100
M: "Thank you.

00:12:53.100 --> 00:12:56.310
Finally Billy, hello sir, at the end"
Billy Boyd (BB): "Hello"

00:12:56.310 --> 00:13:03.019
M: "What a fitting end to this wonderful journey,
with this song: 'The Last Goodbye'.

00:13:03.019 --> 00:13:04.639
How did that come about?"

00:13:04.639 --> 00:13:11.499
BB: "Uhm, I suppose ehm...

00:13:11.499 --> 00:13:12.499
I don't know... no really...

00:13:12.499 --> 00:13:17.610
I just know that I liked when I got the phonecall
and I got the chance to go back to New Zealand,

00:13:17.610 --> 00:13:23.809
and to work with Fran and Philippa so closely
on writing the song.

00:13:23.809 --> 00:13:29.009
Yeah, it's sort of organically happened is
suppose 'cause the song from 'Return of the

00:13:29.009 --> 00:13:34.050
King' was in the trailer, and then we started
talking about doing this song, and it sort

00:13:34.050 --> 00:13:36.439
of just sort of snowballed from there you
know"

00:13:36.439 --> 00:13:37.500
PJ: "But it was...

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:42.999
I mean it was, apart from, you know, the fact
that you've got a fantastic voice, you know,

00:13:42.999 --> 00:13:43.999
we...

00:13:43.999 --> 00:13:44.999
we...

00:13:44.999 --> 00:13:45.999
It was also...

00:13:45.999 --> 00:13:49.350
We felt just as an emotional thing, it was
almost like a handover from the first three

00:13:49.350 --> 00:13:52.879
films to the second set of three films"
BB: "Yeah"

00:13:52.879 --> 00:13:59.779
PJ: "That Pippin sort of jumps in there to...
you know... the beginning of his story I think

00:13:59.779 --> 00:14:00.779
is..."

00:14:00.779 --> 00:14:02.630
Billy: "I was just thinking about that when
you were talking watching it in the right

00:14:02.630 --> 00:14:03.630
order you know so..."

00:14:03.630 --> 00:14:05.220
PJ: "Yeah yeah yeah"
BB: "But it was great.

00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:09.019
It was so good to be down there, you know,
in a different way when you are used to being

00:14:09.019 --> 00:14:14.260
an actor and you leave it to be there at the
this sort of last couple of weeks, and the

00:14:14.260 --> 00:14:18.139
excitement of putting it all together, you
know, and getting to sit with Pete as he edited.

00:14:18.139 --> 00:14:20.050
That was, you know, a real, real treat; it
was great"

00:14:20.050 --> 00:14:21.589
M: "Thank you.

00:14:21.589 --> 00:14:23.589
And the gentleman over here, we'll start with
you"

00:14:23.589 --> 00:14:25.910
Audience Member: "Thank you very much.

00:14:25.910 --> 00:14:28.929
Hello, I'm Marcus from Germany.

00:14:28.929 --> 00:14:36.829
This is a question for Peter Jackson, of cause,
next year we get the Blu-ray and DVD of 'The

00:14:36.829 --> 00:14:40.070
Hobbit 3', but what happens after this?

00:14:40.070 --> 00:14:45.649
Is this really the end of Middle Earth, or
do you see something in the books of Tolkien's

00:14:45.649 --> 00:14:49.119
where you would say that it's worth to do
another movie?

00:14:49.119 --> 00:14:55.689
I mean, we are in the century of franchise"
PJ: "Erm... we have the extended cut of 'The

00:14:55.689 --> 00:14:58.930
Battle of the Five Armies' which is gonna
be a fantastic extended cut, there's some

00:14:58.930 --> 00:15:00.570
really great stuff.

00:15:00.570 --> 00:15:05.019
I go back to New Zealand in the new year and
we work for four to five months putting that

00:15:05.019 --> 00:15:10.170
together, which will be fun, 'cause it's all
gonna be... there's actually some quite good

00:15:10.170 --> 00:15:11.259
stuff in it.

00:15:11.259 --> 00:15:13.420
So I'm looking forward to that, then no.

00:15:13.420 --> 00:15:19.160
Look at that point, I mean it's a legal thing
the Tolkien Estate owns the writings of Professor

00:15:19.160 --> 00:15:20.160
Tolkien.

00:15:20.160 --> 00:15:25.009
The Hobbit and The Lord of the Rings were
sold by Professor Tolkien in the late sixties,

00:15:25.009 --> 00:15:31.209
the film rights, but they are the only two
works of his that have ever been sold as,

00:15:31.209 --> 00:15:34.759
you know, potential films so...

00:15:34.759 --> 00:15:40.589
So without the corporation of the Tolkien
Estate there can't be any more films.

00:15:40.589 --> 00:15:41.699
So yeah..."

00:15:41.699 --> 00:15:45.629
M: "Gentleman in the red t-shirt there"
Audience Member: "Another question for Mr.

00:15:45.629 --> 00:15:46.629
Jackson.

00:15:46.629 --> 00:15:48.399
I was disappointed that I've not yet, I'm
over here"

00:15:48.399 --> 00:15:49.579
Peter: "Ah thank you.

00:15:49.579 --> 00:15:50.749
Hello"
Audience Member: "Hello.

00:15:50.749 --> 00:15:54.149
I'm disappointed that I've not yet had a chance
to see this in 48 frames, but I'm looking

00:15:54.149 --> 00:15:55.499
forward to that.

00:15:55.499 --> 00:16:00.439
How do you feel now about the whole high frame
rate, I mean I don't know if you call that

00:16:00.439 --> 00:16:03.079
an experiment, do you think you continue working
in that format?"

00:16:03.079 --> 00:16:06.959
Peter: "Yeah, I mean I just... look I just
think it's a much better way to see films

00:16:06.959 --> 00:16:12.970
and I... it's, you know, after the first Hobbit
movie I went back in to the grading room and

00:16:12.970 --> 00:16:17.200
I figured out a lot of little tricks to make
it look less like the video thing.

00:16:17.200 --> 00:16:20.699
I know that people criticized it the first
time round and I should have understood why

00:16:20.699 --> 00:16:26.169
when I... so then I did a lot of work so the
second and third Hobbit movies has got a slightly

00:16:26.169 --> 00:16:31.119
different look, a more of a textured look
which I regret I didn't do in the first film,

00:16:31.119 --> 00:16:34.839
but it took a while to figure out.

00:16:34.839 --> 00:16:35.839
Look...

00:16:35.839 --> 00:16:38.709
I... the way I always answer that question,
'cause it's a very fair question, is that

00:16:38.709 --> 00:16:43.930
to say that a hundred years ago, movies were
black and white, they were silent, they were

00:16:43.930 --> 00:16:46.190
16 frames a second okay?

00:16:46.190 --> 00:16:48.660
So a hundred years from now what are they
gonna be?

00:16:48.660 --> 00:16:49.660
I mean...

00:16:49.660 --> 00:16:50.660
I don't know.

00:16:50.660 --> 00:16:55.339
We just do not know what form films are gonna
be in a hundred years time.

00:16:55.339 --> 00:16:59.660
But I think that you can absolutely guarantee,
they're not gonna be 24 frames a second, they're

00:16:59.660 --> 00:17:05.970
not gonna be 2D so... at what point between
now and a hundred years do these advances

00:17:05.970 --> 00:17:06.970
actually happen?

00:17:06.970 --> 00:17:12.320
So just think it's... and ultimately, the
audiences for movies are beginning to dwindle

00:17:12.320 --> 00:17:19.459
is so much competition for seeing things on
iPhones and iPads and everything else that

00:17:19.459 --> 00:17:23.530
it has become a very serious issue for the
industry, 'cause the, you know, the industry

00:17:23.530 --> 00:17:28.950
has to have the audiences in order to makes
these films... to feed itself.

00:17:28.950 --> 00:17:34.320
So it has become a very serious thing for
the industry of how do you get people to leave

00:17:34.320 --> 00:17:38.880
their houses and go to the theater, I mean,
the audiences are slipping away.

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:42.070
So I think it's important that filmmakers
do look at the technology that's available

00:17:42.070 --> 00:17:47.360
and just think of how we can use it to make
that experience in the cinema a little bit

00:17:47.360 --> 00:17:48.360
more exiting.

00:17:48.360 --> 00:17:49.360
So that's my theory."

00:17:49.360 --> 00:17:50.360
M: "Thank you.

00:17:50.360 --> 00:17:51.360
Lady just here"
Audience Member: "Hi, good afternoon.

00:17:51.360 --> 00:17:52.360
This is Tina from Singapore.

00:17:52.360 --> 00:18:00.430
I would like to ask the actors and the directors,
this has to be a journey of self-discovery,

00:18:00.430 --> 00:18:04.910
so for yourself what have you discovered for
yourself as a person or as an actor through

00:18:04.910 --> 00:18:05.910
this journey?"

00:18:05.910 --> 00:18:06.910
M: "Who's that to sorry?"

00:18:06.910 --> 00:18:07.910
Audience Member: "Sorry?"

00:18:07.910 --> 00:18:08.910
M: "Who's that to?

00:18:08.910 --> 00:18:09.910
Who would you like to..."

00:18:09.910 --> 00:18:10.910
Audience Member: "Just whoever that wishes
to answer"

00:18:10.910 --> 00:18:11.910
M: "Who would like to answer that one?"

00:18:11.910 --> 00:18:13.310
PJ: "Self-discovery, who..."

00:18:13.310 --> 00:18:14.310
M: "Self-discovery.

00:18:14.310 --> 00:18:16.710
Martin"
PJ: "I was gonna...

00:18:16.710 --> 00:18:20.980
I thought that you would be the best person"
MF: "Oh really?"

00:18:20.980 --> 00:18:22.680
PJ: "Yeah"
MF: "It's interesting..."

00:18:22.680 --> 00:18:24.390
M: "Martin"
MF: "Oh really?"

00:18:24.390 --> 00:18:29.120
M: "Yeah go Martin"
MF: "I didn't find it really one of self-discovery

00:18:29.120 --> 00:18:30.130
particularly.

00:18:30.130 --> 00:18:36.160
I thoroughly, thoroughly enjoyed it and I
think that's kind of its own worth in a way,

00:18:36.160 --> 00:18:39.070
you know, we do things in life, whether they
are jobs or personal things that you just

00:18:39.070 --> 00:18:43.870
hope will give you enjoyment, and you know,
enrichment in your life, and it certainly

00:18:43.870 --> 00:18:45.130
gave me that.

00:18:45.130 --> 00:18:48.350
I think that I'm still too close to it yet
to see what...

00:18:48.350 --> 00:18:51.150
how it's changed me if it has changed me or
how it's informed me.

00:18:51.150 --> 00:18:53.040
I think, maybe in 15 years I might know.

00:18:53.040 --> 00:18:56.650
I mean genuinely though, very often you know,
things you learn in life, you don't know five

00:18:56.650 --> 00:19:01.140
minutes later, you know them much later so
all I know is that I'm very, very glad I did

00:19:01.140 --> 00:19:04.600
it, I'm very grateful that it was able to
work out and yeah...

00:19:04.600 --> 00:19:08.000
I would have been without it in the last few
years of my life that's for sure"

00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:09.000
M: "Thank you.

00:19:09.000 --> 00:19:12.850
Lady in the front row here, we'll go there"
Audience Member: "Great film everyone; well

00:19:12.850 --> 00:19:14.170
done"
MF: "Thank you"

00:19:14.170 --> 00:19:17.150
Audience Member: "The question is for Lee
and Richard.

00:19:17.150 --> 00:19:22.250
There's obviously a lot of male ego, love/hate
relationship over there.

00:19:22.250 --> 00:19:26.210
Did it affect you on-set relationship in any
way?"

00:19:26.210 --> 00:19:27.530
RA: "Male ego?"

00:19:27.530 --> 00:19:31.970
Audience Member: "Yeah"
PJ: "There's a lot of male ego on this table"

00:19:31.970 --> 00:19:37.290
RA: "It's kind of build into the relationship,
you know, you've got two kings that represent

00:19:37.290 --> 00:19:43.580
two races and so it's essential there is this
standoff between two characters and it's setup

00:19:43.580 --> 00:19:48.990
in the second film, and even thought the characters
don't really come face to face in the third

00:19:48.990 --> 00:19:53.261
piece of the jigsaw puzzle you can really
feel the tension between the elves and the

00:19:53.261 --> 00:19:57.300
dwarves, linchpinned by these two potential
kings, I think"

00:19:57.300 --> 00:19:59.230
Audience Member: "Your on-set relationship?"

00:19:59.230 --> 00:20:06.081
RA: "Yeah I mean we were pretty antagonist,
everytime the elf walked in to the room I

00:20:06.081 --> 00:20:11.500
would bristle"
M: "Can we go... gentleman with the microphone,

00:20:11.500 --> 00:20:19.600
there"
Audience Member: "Hi, this is Tiago from Brazil.

00:20:19.600 --> 00:20:27.060
So this is hashtag #OneLastTime, I would like
to know from everybody, beside yourselves,

00:20:27.060 --> 00:20:32.200
besides Middle Earth, besides working with
each other, what are you gonna miss the most?"

00:20:32.200 --> 00:20:38.480
M: "Can you direct that to a couple of people
please, 'cause we've got a lot of questions

00:20:38.480 --> 00:20:39.620
to get back to"
Audience Member: "Peter Jackson"

00:20:39.620 --> 00:20:44.380
PJ: "I think you've answered the question
and the truth is, I'm gonna miss the fun on

00:20:44.380 --> 00:20:51.570
the set, I mean, as Martin said, I kind of...
making films is very very hard and if you're

00:20:51.570 --> 00:20:56.110
make three of them like this, 266 days of
shooting, it could be bloody hell if it's

00:20:56.110 --> 00:20:58.600
not fun, so I kind of...

00:20:58.600 --> 00:21:03.190
I wanna go to work in the morning and have
fun, I wanna enjoy myself and... fortunately

00:21:03.190 --> 00:21:08.770
this cast is just such a fantastic cast and
they're all very, very funny people and I

00:21:08.770 --> 00:21:13.110
just, you know... outside the fact that the
cameras were rolling and we were all doing

00:21:13.110 --> 00:21:18.040
our part to create the movie, as soon as the
cameras stopped rolling we just...

00:21:18.040 --> 00:21:19.810
I mean, it was just fun.

00:21:19.810 --> 00:21:24.620
It was an enjoyable way to spend the day,
and I'm surely gonna miss that, that's...

00:21:24.620 --> 00:21:26.810
yeah"
M: "Okay, if we can get a microphone down

00:21:26.810 --> 00:21:31.270
the front row and then we do some question
over this side, and then we come back over

00:21:31.270 --> 00:21:33.770
here in a sec"
Audience Member: "Peter, mainly, and Ian,

00:21:33.770 --> 00:21:40.230
and everyone else on the panel that perhaps
has children, I wondered, with J.R.R.

00:21:40.230 --> 00:21:45.580
Tolkien's writings, I'm sure most people in
the room would agree that the books are brilliant.

00:21:45.580 --> 00:21:52.250
Do you think that there's still literature
written for children today, that's as good?"

00:21:52.250 --> 00:21:55.290
PJ: "Do I think there's literature..."

00:21:55.290 --> 00:21:56.760
Philippa: "Yes, absolutely.

00:21:56.760 --> 00:22:02.580
Yes I think... and formed and influenced by
him, and we have... actually have a children's

00:22:02.580 --> 00:22:06.300
book, well wonderful children's book, author
sitting at this table, Evangeline Lilly"

00:22:06.300 --> 00:22:08.730
PJ: "Squickerwonkers"
Philippa: "Squickerwonkers.

00:22:08.730 --> 00:22:13.440
Highly recommend it... who.... is venturing
into that?

00:22:13.440 --> 00:22:15.040
The world of imagination so..."

00:22:15.040 --> 00:22:17.850
PJ: "Yeah, yeah"
M: "Great thank you.

00:22:17.850 --> 00:22:20.780
And if we can go to the gentleman with the
hat in the front row and along to the blue

00:22:20.780 --> 00:22:22.510
coat"
Audience Member: "Hello there., I've got a

00:22:22.510 --> 00:22:28.060
question from my ten year old son, Hag, it's
to whoever is brave enough to answer really.

00:22:28.060 --> 00:22:30.570
There's now six movies, which one's your favorite?"

00:22:30.570 --> 00:22:31.750
All: "uuuuh..."

00:22:31.750 --> 00:22:34.700
EL: "Fellowship"
RA: "Two Towers"

00:22:34.700 --> 00:22:41.190
IM: "Fellowship of the ring"
MF: "I think maybe, Two Towers"

00:22:41.190 --> 00:22:43.460
Audience Member: "I think that enough.

00:22:43.460 --> 00:22:45.830
Let's move on"
PJ: "I like the Battle of Helms Deep actually,

00:22:45.830 --> 00:22:48.440
I mean I like the Two Towers but I like particularly
the Helms Deep sequence.

00:22:48.440 --> 00:22:49.440
I....

00:22:49.440 --> 00:22:50.440
I... yeah I mean... yeah"
Audience Member: "They're all brilliant"

00:22:50.440 --> 00:22:56.190
MF: "Thank you"
M: "Can you pass the mike along please; we'll

00:22:56.190 --> 00:22:59.330
do the lady and then the gentleman in the
blue coat, thank you"

00:22:59.330 --> 00:23:07.630
Audience Member: "Can I ask Peter, given your
daytime immersion in fantasy what do you dream

00:23:07.630 --> 00:23:08.630
about?"

00:23:08.630 --> 00:23:11.760
PB: "Fran Walsh"
Audience Member: "Sorry?"

00:23:11.760 --> 00:23:13.750
PJ: "Well I mean, I...

00:23:13.750 --> 00:23:14.750
I uhm...

00:23:14.750 --> 00:23:15.750
I...

00:23:15.750 --> 00:23:20.440
It's a very boring answer, but every time
I make a movie and these movies were no exception.

00:23:20.440 --> 00:23:26.780
The first day I start shooting, I start having
a recurring nightmare every single night that

00:23:26.780 --> 00:23:33.850
I'm lying in bed and there's a film crew surrounding
the bed waiting for me, to tell them what

00:23:33.850 --> 00:23:38.150
to do, and I don't quite know which movie
I'm actually making.

00:23:38.150 --> 00:23:42.060
And I'm not sure, I don't think there's even
a script, and they're all standing there needing

00:23:42.060 --> 00:23:46.770
to have information from me and I'm in bed,
I'm tired, I'm exhausted, I can barely think

00:23:46.770 --> 00:23:53.150
straight, so I'm sort of... and that nightmare
starts on the clock the first day of shooting

00:23:53.150 --> 00:23:57.590
and it's every night till the last day of
shooting, and then I... then I... then it

00:23:57.590 --> 00:23:58.590
stops.

00:23:58.590 --> 00:24:03.320
So it's hell 'cause I have all day on set,
then I go sleep and it's all bloody night

00:24:03.320 --> 00:24:04.520
on set as well.

00:24:04.520 --> 00:24:06.630
Uhm... so that's what I dream about.

00:24:06.630 --> 00:24:48.810
It does stop, the day we stop shooting it
goes away, and then I have nice dreams"

00:24:48.810 --> 00:25:02.110
M: "Gentleman in the blue coat please.

00:25:02.110 --> 00:25:03.230
Thank you.

00:25:03.230 --> 00:25:10.620
We'll come back to you"
Audience Member: "Uhm... congratulations guys"

00:25:10.620 --> 00:25:19.770
All: "Thank you"
Audience Member: "It was a beautiful movie

00:25:19.770 --> 00:25:53.200
and I can't wait to show my children.

00:25:53.200 --> 00:26:48.320
And it's unprofessional of me 

00:26:48.320 --> 00:26:59.650
to say I had a little cry"
All: "Aaaawh"

00:26:59.650 --> 00:27:15.300
Audience Member: "In terms of the films, particularly
the next generation seeing such powerful stories,

00:27:15.300 --> 00:27:21.490
and this is obviously cinematically telling
Tolkien's story.

00:27:21.490 --> 00:27:29.980
I'm thinking particularly Martin for yourself,
do you feel a sense of pride, that this is

00:27:29.980 --> 00:28:03.040
essentially a story about the little man,
figuratively and literally, who's made a big

00:28:03.040 --> 00:28:12.620
impact on the world, who has a message that's
important to pass on.

00:28:12.620 --> 00:28:31.330
You usually play a guy who's like the everyman,
how did you find putting yourself in this

00:28:31.330 --> 00:28:54.420
fantastical situation, because you weren't
in 

00:28:54.420 --> 00:29:30.790
the other ones, so it's also new for you 
in your life as well?"

